---
import Command from "../../components/Command.astro"
import CommandList from "../../components/CommandList.astro"
import HelpSection from "../../components/HelpSection.astro"
import Help from "../../layouts/Help.astro"
import { site } from "../../site"
---

<Help
    title="Troubleshooting: Rollport"
    description="What to do when Rollport does not open, does not find the iPhone, or cannot finish an import."
>
    <div class="max-w-(--container-text)">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold">Troubleshooting</h1>
    </div>

    <HelpSection question id="mac-cannot-open">
        <p>
            Apple has not notarized Rollport yet, so macOS blocks it the first
            time you open it. Close the message. Then open{" "}
            <b>System Settings</b> › <b>Privacy &amp; Security</b> and click{" "}
            <b>Open Anyway</b>.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="windows-protected">
        <p>
            Rollport is not signed yet, so Microsoft Defender SmartScreen warns
            about it. Click <b>More info</b>, then <b>Run anyway</b>.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="windows-apple-devices">
        <p>
            Windows needs Apple's USB driver to see the iPhone. Install Apple
            Devices from the Microsoft Store, or iTunes. Then connect the iPhone
            again. <a href={site.appleDevices}>Get Apple Devices</a>
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="linux-usbmuxd">
        <p>
            Rollport reaches the iPhone through <code>usbmuxd</code>. Install it
            with your package manager, then connect the iPhone again:
        </p>
        <CommandList
            items={[
                {
                    label: "Debian, Ubuntu",
                    command: "sudo apt install usbmuxd",
                },
                { label: "Fedora", command: "sudo dnf install usbmuxd" },
            ]}
        />
        <p>
            usbmuxd starts when an iPhone is plugged in and stops when the last
            one is unplugged, so until then Rollport asks you to connect the
            iPhone. If Rollport says <b>Cannot see iPhones</b> while the iPhone
            is plugged in, usbmuxd did not start or stopped answering. Restart
            it:
        </p>
        <Command>sudo systemctl restart usbmuxd</Command>
        <p>
            If your usbmuxd listens somewhere else than{" "}
            <code>/var/run/usbmuxd</code>, set{" "}
            <code>USBMUXD_SOCKET_ADDRESS</code> to its socket path or{" "}
            <code>host:port</code>.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="nothing-happens">
        <p>
            Unlock the iPhone. The first time, tap <b>Trust</b>, then enter your
            passcode. If Rollport still does not see it, try another cable or
            another USB port.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="trust-refused">
        <p>
            <b>Don't Trust</b> was tapped on the iPhone, so it does not ask
            again for now. Unplug the iPhone and plug it in again. When it asks
            whether to trust this computer, tap <b>Trust</b>, then enter your
            passcode.
        </p>
        <p>
            If the iPhone does not ask again, open <b>Settings</b> ›{" "}
            <b>General</b> › <b>Transfer or Reset iPhone</b> › <b>Reset</b> ›{" "}
            <b>Reset Location &amp; Privacy</b> on the iPhone.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="cannot-connect">
        <p>
            The iPhone is plugged in, but it did not answer as expected. The red
            text under the message says what went wrong. Unplug the iPhone,
            unlock it, and plug it in again. Rollport keeps trying every few
            seconds. If that does not help, restart the computer and the iPhone.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="camera-roll">
        <p>
            The iPhone trusts this computer, but it is locked, so it does not
            show its photos. Unlock it. Rollport tries again every few seconds
            and continues on its own.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="folder-unusable">
        <p>
            Rollport cannot open or write to the folder. The red text says why.
            Common reasons:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                The folder was moved, renamed, or deleted, or the disk it is on
                is not connected.
            </li>
            <li>You do not have permission to write to the folder.</li>
            <li>
                The folder stopped responding, which happens with network drives
                and sleeping disks.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>rollport-library.db</code> in the folder is damaged.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Connect the disk or fix the folder, then choose it again, or choose
            another folder.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="not-enough-space">
        <p>
            Rollport always leaves 2 GB free on the disk, so the computer keeps
            working. Before an import, it tells you when everything will not fit
            and how much to free up. You can also choose a shorter date range in{" "}
            <b>Options</b>.
        </p>
        <p>
            If the disk fills up during an import, Rollport copies what fits and
            says how much space the rest needs. Free up space, then click{" "}
            <b>Continue</b>.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="not-copied">
        <p>
            Rollport tries each file three times. When a file still cannot be
            copied, Rollport goes on with the others, then names the file that
            failed. The file stays on the iPhone, and the next import tries it
            again.
        </p>
        <p>
            When the import itself fails, for example because the folder cannot
            be written to, Rollport says <b>Import failed</b>. Everything
            imported before that stays in the folder. The red text says what
            went wrong.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="folder-busy">
        <p>
            Only one import at a time can write into a folder, so two imports
            never mix up its record. Rollport is already importing into this
            folder, on this computer or another one. Wait until it is done, then
            import again.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="network-drive">
        <p>
            Rollport can import into a folder on a network drive, but if the
            connection drops, the import stops. What is already copied stays
            there, and you can click <b>Continue</b> later. A folder on an
            internal or external disk is more reliable.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="linux-blank">
        <p>
            Some graphics drivers do not work with WebKitGTK, which draws
            Rollport's window. With the NVIDIA driver, Rollport sets{" "}
            <code>__NV_DISABLE_EXPLICIT_SYNC=1</code> itself, which stops the
            crash on Wayland ("Error 71") and keeps the GPU drawing. A value you
            set yourself wins, so <code>__NV_DISABLE_EXPLICIT_SYNC=0</code>{" "}
            turns this off. The AppImage changes screens without animation,
            because its older WebKitGTK crashes on it.
        </p>
        <p>
            If the window still opens empty, or closes again at once, start
            Rollport from a terminal with these commands, one at a time, until
            one works. They draw without the GPU, so screens change without
            animation:
        </p>
        <Command>WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1 rollport</Command>
        <Command>WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1 rollport</Command>
        <p>
            With the AppImage, run it from its folder instead of{" "}
            <code>rollport</code>, for example:
        </p>
        <Command>WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1 ./Rollport.AppImage</Command>
        <p>
            Please also <a href={`${site.repo}/issues`}>open an issue</a> with
            your Linux distribution and graphics card, so we can fix it for
            everyone.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="windows-heic">
        <p>
            The iPhone saves photos as HEIC and videos as HEVC, and Rollport
            copies them as they are. Windows needs two extensions from the
            Microsoft Store to open them:{" "}
            <a href={site.heif}>HEIF Image Extensions</a> (free) and{" "}
            <a href={site.hevc}>HEVC Video Extensions</a> (about $0.99). The
            photos need both.
        </p>
        <p>
            To take new photos as JPEG instead, open <b>Settings</b> ›{" "}
            <b>Camera</b> › <b>Formats</b> on the iPhone and choose{" "}
            <b>Most Compatible</b>. Photos you already took stay HEIC.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="forget">
        <p>Quit Rollport, then delete its settings folder:</p>
        <CommandList
            items={[
                {
                    label: "Mac",
                    command:
                        "~/Library/Application Support/com.artificiadrian.rollport",
                },
                {
                    label: "Windows",
                    command: "%APPDATA%\\com.artificiadrian.rollport",
                },
                {
                    label: "Linux",
                    command: "~/.config/com.artificiadrian.rollport",
                },
            ]}
        />
        <p>
            That folder holds only the folder you used last, the folder of each
            iPhone, and the window size. Your photos and folders are not
            touched. The next time you open Rollport, it starts as if newly
            installed.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection question id="something-else">
        <p>
            Tell us what happened, which system you use (Mac, Windows, or
            Linux), and your iPhone model.{" "}
            <a href={`${site.repo}/issues`}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>
        </p>
    </HelpSection>
</Help>
